<section>
  <title>Solving the 1D Wave Equation</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      We derived the 1D wave equation in section <xref ref="sect-modeling-pdes" />.
      We will now solve this as an initial value problem.
      Consider
      <me>
        \frac{\partial^{2} u}{\partial{t}^{2}}= c^{2} \frac{\partial^{2} u}{\partial{x}^{2}}
      </me>
      with boundary conditions
      <me>
        \begin{aligned} u(0,t)\amp = u(L,t) = 0, \amp \amp  \text{for $t \geq 0$}\\ \qquad u(x,0)\amp = f(x) \amp \amp  \text{for $0 \leq x \leq L$ }\\ u_{t}(x,0) \amp= 0 \amp \amp  \text{for $0 \leq x \leq L$} \end{aligned}
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      The technique of <term>separation of variables</term> is the standard way to solve this and other linear PDEs.
      First start by assuming that the solution <m>u(x,t)</m> can be written as a product of functions solely of <m>x</m> and <m>t</m> or <m>u(x,t) = X(x) T(t)</m>.
      Then substituting this into the PDE:
      <me>
        \begin{aligned} \frac{\partial^{2} (X(x)T(t))}{\partial{t}^{2}}\amp= c^{2} \frac{\partial^{2} (X(x) T(t))}{\partial{x}^{2}}\\ XT''\amp= c^{2} X'' T \end{aligned}
      </me>
      or
      <me>
        \frac{1}{c^{2}}\frac{T''}{T}= \frac{X''}{ X}
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      Now since <m>T</m> (and therefore <m>T''</m>) depends only on <m>t</m> and <m>X</m> (and also <m>X''</m>) only depends on <m>x</m>, each side of the PDE must be equal to a constant (independent of either <m>x</m> or <m>t</m>).
      Let's say it is <m>-\lambda</m> or
      <md>
        \frac{1}{c^{2}}\frac{T''}{T}= \frac{X''}{X}= -\lambda
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      This results in two equations:
      <md>
        <mrow xml:id="eq-sep-vars-X" number="yes">T'' +c^{2}\lambda T \amp = 0</mrow>
        <mrow xml:id="eq-sep-vars-T" number="yes">X'' + \lambda X \amp= 0 </mrow>
      </md>
      The boundary conditions are <m>u(0) = u(L) = 0</m> for all <m>t</m>, so substituting <m>u=XT</m> we get
      <me>
        X(0)T(t) = X(L) T(t)= 0
      </me>
      for all <m>t</m> or <m>X(0)=X(L)=0</m>.
      The ODE for <m>X</m> is that in <xref ref="eq-sep-vars-X" /> with these boundary conditions.
      This is a Sturm-Liouville problem that was solved in example <xref ref="ex-sturm-liouville" />.
      We know that the only form of the solution is when <m>\lambda \gt 0</m> and from the problem that we solved above we know that
      <men xml:id="eq-sep-vars-eigenvalues">
        \lambda= \frac{n^{2} \pi^{2}}{L^{2}}
      </men>
    </p>

    <p>
      and the solution for <m>X</m> are the eigenfunctions of the problem:
      <me>
        X_{n}(x) = \sin \frac{n \pi x}{L}
      </me>
      for <m>n=1,2,3,\ldots</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Next, we need to solve <xref ref="eq-sep-vars-T" /> and using the eigenvalues in <xref ref="eq-sep-vars-eigenvalues" />
      <me>
        T'' + c^{2} \frac{n^{2} \pi^{2}}{L^{2}}T = 0
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      The solution to this is found by assuming <m>T(t) = e^{r t}</m> and getting the characteristic equation
      <me>
        r^{2} + c^{2} \frac{n^{2} \pi^{2}}{L^{2}}= 0
      </me>
      and thus
      <me>
        r = \pm i \frac{n c \pi}{L}
      </me>
      and the solution to this is
      <me>
        T_{n}(t) = a_{n} \cos \biggl( \frac{n c \pi }{L}\biggr) t + b_{n} \sin \biggl( \frac{n c \pi }{L}\biggr) t
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      We know then that the following is a solution to the PDE:
      <me>
        u_{n}(x,t)= X_{n}(x) T_{n}(t) = \biggl( a_{n} \cos \biggl( \frac{n c \pi }{L}\biggr) t + b_{n} \sin \biggl( \frac{n c \pi }{L}\biggr) t \biggr) \sin \frac{n \pi x}{L}
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      The <term>principle of superposition</term> states that a sum of these solutions is also a solution to the original PDE and in fact the most general solution is
      <me>
        \begin{aligned} u(x,t)\amp= \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}u_{n}(x,t) \\\amp= \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\biggl( a_{n} \cos \biggl( \frac{n c \pi }{L}\biggr) t + b_{n} \sin \biggl( \frac{n c \pi }{L}\biggr) t \biggr) \sin \frac{n \pi x}{L} \end{aligned}
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      Lastly, we use the initial conditions <m>u(x,0)=f(x)</m> and <m>u_{t}(x,0)=0</m> for <m>0 \leq x \leq L</m> to solve for <m>a_{n}</m> and <m>b_{n}</m> above.
      We will look at the second condition first:
      <me>
        u_{t}(x,t)= \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{n c \pi}{L}\biggl( -a_{n} \sin \biggl( \frac{n c \pi }{L}\biggr) t + b_{n} \cos \biggl( \frac{n c \pi }{L}\biggr) t \biggr) \sin \frac{n \pi x}{L}
      </me>
      and evaluated at <m>t=0</m>:
      <me>
        u_{t}(x,0) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{n c \pi}{L}\biggl( 0 + b_{n} \biggr) \sin \frac{n \pi x}{L}
      </me>
      and since <m>u_{t}(x,0)\equiv 0</m>, then <m>b_{n}=0</m>.
      The first condition:
      <me>
        u(x,0) = f(x) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}a_{n} \sin \frac{n \pi x}{L}
      </me>
      is the Fourier sine series for <m>0 \leq x \leq L</m>.
      Thus
      <me>
        a_{n} = \frac{2}{L}\int_{0}^{L} f(x) \sin \frac{n \pi x}{L}\, dx
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      We summarize the solution to the wave equation.
    </p>

    <p>
      The solution to the wave equation
      <me>
        u_{tt}=c^{2} u_{xx}
      </me>
      with boundary conditions
      <me>
        u(0,t) = u(L,t) = 0
      </me>
      and initial conditions
      <me>
        \begin{aligned} u(x,0)\amp= f(x), \amp u_{t}(x,0)\amp= 0 \end{aligned}
      </me>
      is
      <me>
        u(x,t)= \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}a_{n} \cos \biggl( \frac{n c \pi }{L}\biggr) t \sin \frac{n \pi x}{L}
      </me>
      where
      <me>
        a_{n}= \frac{2}{L}\int_{0}^{L} f(x) \sin \frac{n\pi x}{L}\, dx
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      The following two examples show specific solutions for given functions <m>f(x)</m>.
    </p>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the specific solution to the wave equation if <m>f(x) = 0.1 \sin (\pi x/L)</m>
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Specifically, we only need to find the coefficients <m>a_{n}</m> above:
          <me>
            \begin{aligned} a_{n}\amp= \frac{2}{L}\int_{0}^{L} 0.1 \sin \frac{\pi x}{L}\sin \frac{n\pi x}{L}\, dx \\\amp= \begin{cases}0.1 \qquad \text{if $n=1$}\\ 0 \qquad \text{otherwise}\end{cases} \end{aligned}
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          Thus the full solution to the PDE is:
          <me>
            u(x,t)= 0.1 \sin \frac{\pi x}{L}\cos \frac{\pi t}{L}
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the solution to the wave equation above if the initial shape of the string is
          <me>
            \begin{aligned} f(x)\amp= \begin{cases}kx,\amp\text{if $0\leq x&#x3C; L/2$}\\ k(L-x),\amp\text{if $L/2 \leq x \leq L$}\end{cases} \end{aligned}
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          As before, we only need to find the coefficients of <m>a_{n}</m> given by
          <me>
            \begin{aligned} a_{n}\amp= \frac{2}{L}\int_{0}^{L} f(x) \sin \frac{n \pi x}{L}\, dx \\\amp= \frac{2}{L}\int_{0}^{L/2}kx \sin \frac{n \pi x}{L}\, dx + \frac{2}{L}\int_{L/2}^{L} k(L-x) \sin \frac{n \pi x}{L}\, dx \\\amp= \begin{cases}\frac{4k L}{n^2 \pi^2}(-1)^{(n-1)/2},\amp\text{if $n$ is odd}\\ 0,\amp\text{otherwise}\end{cases} \end{aligned}
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          And thus the full solution to the wave equation is:
          <me>
            \begin{aligned} u(x,t)\amp= \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}a_{n} \sin \biggl(\frac{n \pi x}{L}\biggr) \sin \biggl(\frac{n \pi t}{L}\biggr) \\\amp= \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{4 k L (-1)^{n+1}}{\pi^{2} (2n-1)^{2}}\sin \biggl( \frac{(2n-1) \pi x}{L}\biggr) \sin \biggl(\frac{(2n-1) \pi t}{L}\biggr) \end{aligned}
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>
  </introduction>


  <subsection>
    <title>Solution to the Wave Equation with a free boundary condition</title>

    <p>
      We now look at the wave equation with what is termed a free boundary condition.
      Consider
      <me>
        \frac{\partial^{2} u}{\partial{t}^{2}}= c^{2} \frac{\partial^{2} u}{\partial{x}^{2}}
      </me>
      with boundary conditions
      <men xml:id="eq-wave-bc-free">
        \begin{aligned} u(0,t)\amp= 0 \qquad u_{x}(L,t) = 0, \qquad \text{for $t \geq 0$} \end{aligned}
      </men>
      and initial conditions
      <md>
        <mrow xml:id="eq-wave-free-ic1" number="yes"> u(x,0)\amp= f(x) \amp \amp  \text{for $0 \leq x \leq L$ }</mrow>
        <mrow xml:id="eq-wave-free-ic2" number="yes">u_{t}(x,0)\amp= 0 \amp \amp  \text{for $0 \leq x \leq L$}</mrow>
      </md>
      and the difference between this and the initial value problem at the beginning of the section is the boundary condition at <m>x=L</m>.
      Above, the function <m>u</m> was forced to be 0 there and in this case, the derivative is 0 at <m>x=L</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Using separation of variables by assuming that <m>u(x,t)=X(x)T(t)</m>, and substituting into the wave equation and dividing through by <m>c^{2} XT</m>, we again get the equation
      <md>
        \frac{1}{c^{2}}\frac{T''}{T} = \frac{X''}{X}= -\lambda^{2}
      </md>
      where again since each term only depends on <m>t</m> or <m>x</m>, it must be a constant, which we say is <m>-\lambda</m>.
      The differential equation in <m>X</m> is
      <md>
        \begin{aligned} X'' + \lambda X\amp= 0\amp X(0)\amp= 0, X'(L)=0 \end{aligned}
      </md>
      where the boundary conditions came from <xref ref="eq-wave-bc-free" />.
      The solution to this Sturm-Liouville problem is similar to those in Examples <xref ref="ex-sturm-liouville" /> and <xref ref="ex-sturm-liouville-2" />, however the details of this are not shown.
      The eigenfunctions and eigenvalues of this are
      <me>
        \begin{aligned} X_{n}(x)\amp= \sin \lambda_{n} x\amp\lambda_{n}\amp= \frac{(2n-1)\pi}{2L} \end{aligned}
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      Next, we seek the solution to <m>T''+c^{2} \lambda^{2} T = 0</m> which again, similar to solution of the wave equation with fixed boundary conditions, is
      <me>
        T= a \cos c \lambda t + b \sin c \lambda t
      </me>
      which has a solution for each value of <m>n</m> or
      <me>
        T_{n} = a_{n} \cos c \frac{(2n-1)\pi}{2L}t + b_{n} \sin c \frac{(2n-1)\pi}{2L}t
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      Using the principle of superposition, the sum of solutions is also a solution, so
      <me>
        u(x,t) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\biggl(a_{n} \cos c \frac{(2n-1)\pi}{2L}t + b_{n} \sin c \frac{(2n-1)\pi}{2L}t \biggr) \sin \frac{(2n-1)\pi x}{2L}
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      To find the particular solution, we need to find the constants <m>a_{n}</m> and <m>b_{n}</m> which can be found using the initial conditions of the problem.
      Similar to the problem above with the fixed boundary conditions, we will use the initial condition in <xref ref="eq-wave-free-ic2" /> first, which requires that we know the derivative.
      And
      <men xml:id="eq-wave-free-eq1">
        u_{t}= \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}c \lambda_{n} \biggl(-a_{n} \sin c \frac{(2n-1)\pi}{2L}t + b_{n} \cos c \frac{(2n-1)\pi}{2L}t \biggr) \sin \frac{(2n-1)\pi x}{2L}
      </men>
    </p>

    <p>
      Applying the initial condition in <xref ref="eq-wave-free-ic2" />,
      <md>
        u_{t}(x,0)=0 = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}b_{n} c \lambda_{n} \sin \frac{(2n-1)\pi x}{2L}
      </md>
      which implies that <m>b_{n}=0</m> for all <m>n</m>.
      Next, if we substitute this into <xref ref="eq-wave-free-eq1" />) and apply the boundary condition in (<xref ref="eq-wave-free-ic1" />,
      <me>
        u(x,0)= f(x) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}a_{n} \sin \frac{(2n-1)\pi x}{2L}
      </me>
      and the coefficients can be found using Theorem <xref ref="thm-sturm-liouville" />, to be
      <me>
        \begin{aligned} a_{n}\amp= \frac{\langle f(x), X_{n}(x) \rangle}{\langle X_{n}(x), X_{n}(x) \rangle}\\\amp= \frac{\int_{0}^{L} f(x) \sin (2n-1)\pi x/(2L) \, dx }{\int_{0}^{L} \sin^{2} (2n-1)\pi x/(2L) \, dx }\\\amp= \frac{2}{L}\int_{0}^{L} f(x) \sin \frac{(2n-1)\pi x}{2L}\, dx \end{aligned}
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      To summarize, the solution to the 1D wave equation
      <me>
        \frac{\partial^{2} u}{\partial{t}^{2}}= c^{2} \frac{\partial^{2} u}{\partial{x}^{2}}
      </me>
      with boundary conditions
      <me>
        u(0,t) = 0, \quad u_{x}(L,t) = 0, \qquad \text{for $t \geq 0$}
      </me>
      and initial conditions
      <me>
        \begin{aligned} u(x,0)\amp= f(x) \qquad \text{for $0 \leq x \leq L$ }\\ u_{t}(x,0)\amp= 0 \qquad \text{for $0 \leq x \leq L$} \end{aligned}
      </me>
      is
      <me>
        u(x,t)= \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}a_{n} \cos c \frac{(2n-1)\pi}{2L}t \sin \frac{(2n-1)\pi x}{2L}
      </me>
      where
      <men xml:id="eq-wave-free-fourier-coeffs">
        a_{n}= \frac{2}{L}\int_{0}^{L} f(x) \sin \frac{(2n-1)\pi x}{2L}\, dx
      </men>
    </p>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the solution to the 1D wave equation with free boundary condition at <m>x=1</m> if
          <me>
            f(x)= x^{2}\qquad\text{for $0 \leq x \leq 1$}
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          In this case, we only need to find the constants <m>a_{n}</m> as defined in <xref ref="eq-wave-free-fourier-coeffs" />,
          <me>
            \begin{aligned}
            a_{n}\amp= 2 \int_{0}^{1} x^{2} \sin \frac{(2n-1)\pi x}{2}\, dx \\\amp= \frac{(-1)^{n} (8\pi + 16\pi n) -16}{(2n-1)^{3} \pi^{3}}
            \end{aligned}
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          So the full solution to this wave equation is
        </p>
        <md>
          u(x,t)= \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^{n} (8\pi + 16\pi n) -16}{(2n-1)^{3} \pi^{3}}\cos c \frac{(2n-1)\pi}{2L}t \sin \frac{(2n-1)\pi x}{2L}
        </md>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>
</section>